{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"rebar-debug on 895c (Bedrock grammar failure): ROOT CAUSE PROVEN, fix up for review as Gerrit 1442; aec1 landed (ff4c56007a) and closed.\n\n== 895c ROOT CAUSE (proven, not inferred) ==\nStory 18ae (commit 6c8daf7945, 08-05 00:48) added an exact-model-id row to _MODEL_ID_CAPABILITY_OVERRIDES setting native_structured_output=True for us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6. It is applied AFTER the _REBAR_OVERRIDES family rule (_is_claude -> False) and WINS, flipping the model from PromptedOutput to NativeOutput. rebar then sent Bedrock each contract's JSON Schema for constrained decoding. Anthropic documents a 180s grammar-compilation timeout and a 24-optional-parameter cap; the verification contracts carry 22-36 optional parameters (every field declares default=, so every field is optional in JSON Schema). Compilation exceeds the ceiling -> HTTP 400 ValidationException.\n\nDiscriminating experiment (live us-east-1, serial, one variable at a time):\n  tiny 2-field contract, NATIVE            -> OK      2.0s\n  plan_review_verification (31 opt) NATIVE -> FAIL  185.3s (400)\n  plan_review_verification, PROMPTED       -> OK     11.2s\n  same schema, descriptions stripped (8719->2403 B), NATIVE -> FAIL 59.8s\nSize ladder: review_result 10 opt OK 14.0s | completion_verdict 14 opt OK 29.0s | verification 22 opt FAIL 185.3s | plan_review_verification 31 opt FAIL | code_review_verification 36 opt FAIL 185.4s. Boundary sits between 14 and 22 optionals. BYTE SIZE IS NOT THE PREDICTOR.\n\nTWO failure shapes, same cause, split by botocore version:\n  A: newer botocore (1.43.x) -> server-side 400, 180.6-185.9s, \"Grammar compilation timed out\" / \"Schema is too complex\"\n  B: botocore 1.40.61 (the MAIN CHECKOUT) -> client-side reject in 0.0s, 'Unknown parameter in input: \"outputConfig\"', for EVERY contract however small (ticket_digest is 722 B), and SILENT (the run continued with \"no overlap findings\")\nSo the failure is CHECKOUT-dependent, not ticket-dependent: make-worktree venvs get A, the main checkout gets B.\n\nBlast radius: BOTH gates. code-review.yaml pins its Pass-2 verify to `model: standard` (= sonnet-4-6) binding code_review_verification (36 opt), which FAILS at 185.4s. The project default [llm] model is opus-4-8, a deliberate NON-row that stays prompted -- which is why only standard-pinned steps failed and everything else looked fine.\n\n== FIX (Gerrit 1442, commit 7ac5e7bece, NOT YET MERGED) ==\noutput_mode now gates NativeOutput on (a) whether the installed stack can send outputConfig at all (cached, never raises, fails closed), then (b) a measured complexity bound of 16 optional / 12 union, inside the measured 14-passes/22-fails gap. A BOUND, not a disable: exactly the 3 measured-failing contracts downgrade; the other 16 stay native. The counting walker dereferences $defs and descends items/nested properties -- load-bearing, because VerificationOutput has ONE top-level property, so a top-level-only count would score these contracts at 0-1 and silently fix nothing. failure.py adds translate_schema_complexity_rejection classifying both shapes distinctly (returns UnretryableOutputError; note LLMConfigError, the sibling translator's choice, IS a subclass of LLMUnavailableError so it was unusable here). structured_run falls back to the existing prompted path. Sizes: structured.py 683, structured_run.py 770/800, failure.py 657.\n\nGrounded in OSS practice (researched): the prevailing framework posture is DON'T send a native grammar by default -- langchain-aws defaults method=\"function_calling\", instructor Mode.TOOLS, llama_index FunctionCallingProgram, DSPy re-raises before its JSON fallback, BAML declines native entirely, pydantic-ai ships NO fallback at all. TanStack/ai hit a near-identical case (~8KB, 50+ props), implemented an auto-fallback and STRIPPED IT before merge (hides the signal / swaps strict for non-strict / brittle error-string matching). Variant B vindicates that warning first-hand: its message shares NO vocabulary with variant A, so a string-matching fallback would have missed it entirely. Hence preflight is load-bearing here and the fallback is only a net.\n\n== VALIDATION ==\nHeld-out oracle (tests/unit/test_schema_complexity_gate.py) written RED-first, moved OUT of the implementer's tree, never seen by it: 6/6 GREEN after the fix. TWO defect-seeded mutations each returned it to RED (bypass the complexity bound -> the 2 contract tests red while the other 4 stay green, i.e. discriminating not blanket-failing; remove the variant-B rule -> exactly that test red). The single full-suite failure (test_full_backend_account_is_present) verified PRE-EXISTING on a stashed clean tree. HONESTY NOTE written into the test file: the 2 variant-B cases were added AFTER the implementation, so they are mutation-verified rather than RED-first.\nREPLICATION: review-plan on 895c previously -> INDETERMINATE, verify_failed=true, blocking 0, indeterminate 6, 1 grammar 400. With the fix -> 0 grammar 400s, 0 prerequisite-verifier failures, and a real merit verdict. The original report no longer reproduces.\n\n== OPERATOR WORKAROUND (still required until 1442 merges) ==\n  export REBAR_LLM_STANDARD_MODEL=\"bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8\"\n  export REBAR_LLM_TRIVIAL_MODEL=\"bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8\"\nValidated end-to-end on the same ticket: unmitigated -> 1 grammar 400 + INDETERMINATE; with it -> 0 grammar 400s + BLOCK (a real verdict). CORRECTION issued mid-session: my first version said leave TRIVIAL alone (justified on schema size) -- wrong for variant B, where every native call fails regardless of size. Drop-the-workaround signal: `git show origin/main:src/rebar/llm/structured.py | grep -c native_output_within_bound` (0 = keep it). On the main checkout the real remedy is upgrading botocore 1.40.61 -> >=1.43.64.\n\n== aec1 (instrumentation) LANDED + CLOSED ==\nGerrit 1435 merged as ff4c56007a (LLM-Review +1, Verified +1), submitted as RebarBotNava. All 25 acceptance criteria independently verified against the MERGED tree from a throwaway worktree at that commit -- including producing the rendered `summarize` output that was genuinely missing from the ticket. FORCE-CLOSED (class preexisting) on operator authorization because the completion verifier LOOPS: requests=240/240, tool_calls=253, distinct=30 (ratio 0.119), max_consecutive_repeat=217, ONE search_files signature repeated 222 times, 753.6s. That is c827, not a property of aec1 (no descendants, 4 declared files) -- so the loop is NOT scale-driven. Recorded on c827 and 70bc; 70bc's proposed windowed distinct-ratio (trip <= 0.50) WOULD have caught this at 0.119.\n\n== MY OWN CORRECTIONS THIS SESSION (recorded so they are not repeated) ==\n1. WITHDRAWN: my earlier \"correlates with finding count, threshold between 10 and 15\" measurement on 895c. Real incidence was 27 live 400s across ~9 review targets, not 2 tickets. Two errors caused it: generalising from a hand-picked set of 16 logs, and inferring \"14 runs succeeded\" from the ABSENCE of a failure line -- success logs at INFO and those captures are WARNING-only, so a success could never have appeared. An empty scan is not evidence until the scan itself is checked.\n2. CORRECTED: my RCA overstated severity (\"the review lands INDETERMINATE and unsigned\", \"cannot be claimed\"). A sibling session proved a review signing 3 seconds AFTER that step failed. The failing step is prerequisite_verify specifically, and it blocks certification only when the degraded prerequisite findings are load-bearing. Both observations are true -- different runs. I demonstrated both poles: 895c (has prerequisite c827) -> INDETERMINATE; aec1 run 5 -> PASS.\n3. CORRECTED: the TRIVIAL-class workaround advice above.\n\n== TICKETS FILED (residuals; link discovered_from 895c once its review is idle) ==\n2391-a941-69ff-4645 overlap-judge failing at 312-381s with requests=3 -- a SECOND mode, separable from 895c on three axes (different step, non-overlapping duration band, completed requests vs requests=0). Do not conflate.\n2876-7958-8dee-4882 the direct-Anthropic fallback arm 401s in 0.4s, so a Bedrock rejection has no runtime rescue; the machinery implies redundancy that does not exist. First task is deciding whether that arm is even meant to be reachable (rebar.toml says the bedrock arm deliberately blanks ANTHROPIC_API_KEY).\n4136-a38d-7b33-4b9d a verified ticket description write SILENTLY REVERTED to an older revision while that ticket's own review was in flight; optimistic concurrency did not fire and READ-BACK VERIFICATION PASSED before the loss. Secondhand (reported by the aec1 session), filed anyway because it is silent data loss that invalidates the standard mitigation every agent is told to use.\nDecision recorded on 895c: AC3 (partial credit for a failed verify) closed WON'T FIX per operator -- the degrade is deliberate, and signing over unverified findings would weaken what a plan-review attestation means.\n\n== OPEN / NEXT ==\nGerrit 1442 awaiting LLM-Review + Verified. 895c stays in_progress until 1442 MERGES, then close (a bug close needs --class; `regression` is the right class -- it worked before 6c8daf7945). Then link 2391/2876/4136 discovered_from 895c. Worktrees to retire: /Users/joeoakhart/dbg-895c (holds the change) and /Users/joeoakhart/close-aec1 (throwaway, safe to remove)."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785962621339081001,"uuid":"cd41e536-cb7d-4ed3-b6e1-756aa71c4bd1"}